# `golem/snapshot`

`snapshot.Capture` (space 0) and `CaptureSpaced` (entities paired with their space, e.g. `Registry.AppendSpaced`) capture bytes synchronously for worker-owned writes: `captureRecords` fills one pre-sized slot per entity by index and compacts in order, so the output does not depend on scheduling, and `encode` grows its `pb.Writer` once to the final size. The bytes are complete at return (entities may reuse the buffers `FullUpdate` returned; never defer the encoding to the writer goroutine, `TestContentSnapshotPinsAndFreezesState` and `TestSaveSnapshotCapturesSpaceAndStateTogether` pin this). From `parallelCaptureMin` (4,096) entities, `fillParallel` calls `FullUpdate`/`IsPersistent` from up to `GOMAXPROCS` goroutines (at least `parallelCaptureGrain` = 1,024 entities each) while the caller waits, returns the error of the lowest entity index like a serial pass and re-raises an entity panic on the caller; the thresholds are variables only so tests can force either path. `BenchmarkSaveSnapshotCallerTime` (root package) tracks the caller-blocked time and allocations. and `WriteAsync(path, data)` writes captured bytes atomically in a background goroutine (unique `os.CreateTemp(dir, ".<base>-*.tmp")` in the destination directory, write, Sync, Close, chmod 0644, Rename, remove on failure). Inside the chain, the first write to a destination in a process calls `sweepLeftovers`, which removes that destination's leftover `.<base>-<digits>.tmp` files from a crashed process (digits only, so the temporary file of a destination named `<base>-1` is never matched; the swept set is capped at `maxSweptPaths`). Writes to one destination are ordered by a package-level last-writer chain (`writeChains`: cleaned absolute path → done channel of the latest write; `WriteAsync` swaps its channel in on the caller, the goroutine waits on the previous one, writes, closes its own and deletes the entry if still last), so the file ends with the last call's bytes; never return to a shared `path + ".tmp"`; `Decode` accepts versions 1 and 2 and validates already-read bytes with checked varints/lengths, bounded record allocation and no trailing data. Snapshots whose persistent entities are all in space 0 stay byte-identical version 1; any non-zero space writes version 2. `Record.Space` carries the space (`json:",omitempty"`, so space-0 physics checkpoint JSON is unchanged). Content-pinned entity saves are separate host manifests in `golem/content_snapshot.go`: capture on the tick, write immutable hashed data before replacing the manifest, preserve prior data, verify the exact schema/content/data identity before strict restoration. Consumer restore callbacks must also validate every definition ID/factory; never use defaults to replace restored mutable state. Physics-configured worlds retain paired recovery instead of entity-only content saves.

`Server.LoadSnapshotStrict` in `golem/snapshot_strict.go` is an additive startup-only entity snapshot path. It reads records once, rejects invalid/duplicate/existing identities, propagates every restore error (including unknown types and missing definitions), and validates non-nil entity ID/type matches before server mutation. Successful candidates are bound, receive schema colliders and are published in their recorded space as one provisional registry batch without `OnSpawn`/`OnRemove`. Existing distinct entities remain intact and the ID counter advances without moving backward. Restore callbacks decode mutable state without applying creation defaults or changing live server state. Hosts rebuild transient dependencies after success and before accepting clients/Run. Any configured physics world, in any space, rejects this path (as it does `SaveContentSnapshot`/`LoadContentSnapshot`; surface navigation alone does not); legacy `SaveSnapshot`/`SaveSnapshotOnTick`/`LoadSnapshot` do not check and save or recreate entities only, never physics or navigation state. Paired physics recovery remains independent and covers every serviced space (see below). Legacy `LoadSnapshot` keeps its skip-on-restore-error and ordinary creation behavior.

Portable physics payloads use `CheckpointVersion = 2`, retaining fixed native solver configuration (`Substeps`, `SolverVelocityIterations`, `SolverRelaxIterations`; defaults 4, 1, 1 at 20 Hz). The paired root `PhysicsCheckpoint` envelope is version 1 while only space 0 has physics/navigation and version 2 otherwise (see multi-space checkpoints below). Check embedded physical versions before strict full decoding so old refinement fields produce an explicit unsupported-version/state-migration error, not silent default substitution. Captured controller/body policy and pending forces remain part of stable-ID reconstruction. This migration does not change the entity-only binary snapshot versions, schema fingerprints, portable authoring source v1 or the independently versioned debug format.

Pending physics/navigation edits require separate accepted and desired captures. `PhysicsCheckpoint.PendingWorld` preserves stable pending change metadata, including physical activation already performed under quarantine. Default restoration rejects it without an exact-identity private `PreparedPhysicsRecovery` pair; never substitute the desired navigation capture for accepted geometry silently. Host source reconstruction migrates dynamic state by stable IDs; root validates binding/lease identities and keeps readiness closed through publication or coherent retry.

Entity-only snapshot behavior remains separate from root `PhysicsCheckpoint`. The latter captures all authoritative entities (with owner and non-zero `Record.Space`, restored through `RestoreEntry`) together with physical state, source ownership and navigation intents; it writes hashed data then the manifest, reconstructs private candidates before publication and uses hook-free registry replacement. Do not route paired recovery through `LoadSnapshot` or ordinary `CreateEntity` spawn effects. Hosts must stop connection acceptance and gameplay for the whole recovery operation; explicit recovery hooks rebuild ephemeral membership after readiness.

**Multi-space paired checkpoints.** Capture emits envelope version 1, byte-identical to single-space releases (pinned by `golem/testdata/physics_checkpoint_v1.golden.json`), iff the only space with physics or surface navigation is space 0; entity records of service-less spaces may still carry a non-zero `Record.Space` in version 1. Otherwise it emits version 2: every top-level physical field (Physics through Trajectories, except Entities) stays zero and `Spaces []PhysicsSpaceCheckpoint` holds one section per serviced space in strictly ascending order (a lone space-0 section is non-canonical and rejected). `Spaces` is `omitempty`, so version 1 never gains a key. Validation is per section through `PhysicsCheckpoint.sections()` (version 1's top-level fields are space 0's section): `Physics.ID`, `Navigation.CheckpointID` and `AcceptedNavigation.CheckpointID` equal `CheckpointID`; body content IDs are unique per world only (`p.Checkpoint.Validate`); attachments, navigation agents and trajectories must name entities recorded in the section's space; a navigation agent is staged iff its entity has a body binding (a staged agent without a body would fault the physics step); intents of both `Navigation` and `AcceptedNavigation` must name the section's navigation agents with the same profile (version 1 shares the same per-section validation); entities of section-less spaces carry no services; object/byte limits apply across sections. `DecodePhysicsCheckpoint` checks each section's embedded physics version before strict decoding. Every serviced space needs a physics world; per-space capture options and restore fingerprints/hooks use `PhysicsCheckpointOptions.Spaces` / `PhysicsRestoreOptions.Spaces` (non-zero keys only; top-level fields stay space 0's). `PreparedPhysicsRecovery` is per space: space 0's legacy `PreparePendingWorld(PhysicsCheckpoint)` receives a version-1-shaped view of its section in version 2, other spaces' hooks receive `(checkpoint, section)`. Restore first reports configuration errors (no physics or surface navigation, or a serviced space without physics) like capture, before `ErrBusy` checks. It requires the checkpoint's section set to equal the server's serviced spaces, prepares every space privately (worlds, leases, then entities, bindings, trajectories, schema collider removal/registration checks on each recorded space's layers via `checkColliderRemove` / `planColliderAdd`, navigation configs and builds), then commits each space's navigation and physics in ascending order. Any failure before the first successful commit restores prior readiness everywhere; a failure after one faults every space and keeps physics and navigation readiness closed until a coherent retry. Publication restores entities into their recorded spaces (schema colliders on those spaces' layers), installs each space's bindings, reattaches navigation in each space, restores intents per space, completes all navigation barriers, and resets session spaces (`sentSpace`, `sessionSpaceExplicit`, transition/ack state) with interest/visibility.

**Entity snapshot save and load.** `Save(entities []registry.Entity, fingerprint, path string) <-chan error` iterates entities, skips those where `IsPersistent()` returns false (via the `Persister` interface), calls authoritative `FullUpdate()` on each survivor (owner-only vars are included; public replication redaction does not apply) and encodes a binary file in memory, all synchronously on the caller; only the atomic temp-file-rename write runs in a background goroutine (`WriteAsync`). The channel receives exactly one value (nil or error); a serialization error is delivered without starting a write. `SaveSpaced(entities []registry.SpacedEntity, ...)` is the same with spaces. Never move serialization back into the goroutine: a space move or state change between the call and the write would then save a record's old space with its new state. `Load(path, expectedFingerprint string) ([]Record, error)` validates magic bytes (`GLM\x01`), file format version (1 or 2), and the stored schema fingerprint; returns `ErrFingerprintMismatch` (sentinel error) when the fingerprint differs so callers can fail fast before touching entity state. `Record` holds `EntityID int64`, `TypeName string`, `StateData []byte` (raw `FullUpdate()` bytes, proto-encoded `*XState`) and `Space registry.SpaceID` (0 for version-1 files). Visibility attributes do not affect the schema fingerprint.

**File format** (sequential, no padding): 4-byte magic `G L M 0x01` · varint version · varint `int64` Unix nanosecond timestamp (informational) · length-prefixed `string` fingerprint · varint record count · per record: varint `int64` entity ID · (version 2 only) varint `uint32` space · length-prefixed `string` type name · length-prefixed `bytes` state data. Integers use the `golem/pb` `Writer` varint helpers; `Decode` reads them with checked `binary.Uvarint`.

**`Persister` interface** (`IsPersistent() bool`): implemented by generated `Synced*` types when `persistent:` is set in the entity schema. Entities not implementing `Persister` default to persistent (safe). `IsPersistent()` is generated in `server.go.tmpl`.

**Integration with codegen:** `codegen.ComputeSchemaFingerprint` produces the SHA-256 fingerprint wired into `SharedData.Fingerprint` and emitted as `const SchemaFingerprint` in `shared.go.tmpl`. The fingerprint encodes only **entity type names** and the **(tag → protoType) mapping** per variable—variable names and sync modes are excluded. This means renaming a variable, adding/removing variables, or changing sync/persistent settings does NOT change the fingerprint; only reassigning a tag number, changing a variable's type, or renaming an entity type will. The generated `RestoreEntity(rec snapshot.Record) (golem.Entity, error)` in `shared.go.tmpl` switches on `rec.TypeName`, constructs a zero entity, calls the generated `ApplyState(*XState)` method (symmetric inverse of `FullUpdate`), and returns the live entity. Generated `Register{Name}Wrapper(func(*Synced{Name}) golem.Entity)` hooks can override restore-time return values per entity type; wrappers should be registered during startup or `init`, before `LoadSnapshot` runs. `ApplyState` sets all fields from the state proto and clears dirty bits. Pass `synced.SchemaFingerprint` to both `SaveSnapshot` and `Load`.

**Convenience methods on `golem.Server`:** `Server.SaveSnapshot(fingerprint, path string) <-chan error` (in `golem/snapshot_api.go`) captures with `snapshot.CaptureSpaced(s.reg.AppendSpaced(nil), fingerprint)` on the caller and writes through the package-level `writeSnapshotAsync` (= `snapshot.WriteAsync`, replaced by tests to hold the write); call it on the tick goroutine (tick callback or `Post`) or before `Run` for a tick-coherent save. `Server.SaveSnapshotOnTick(fingerprint, path)` is the any-goroutine form: while running it appends a request to `snapshotRequests` (guarded by `lifeMu`, not the droppable post queue), which `drainSnapshotRequests` captures with `SaveSnapshot` on the tick goroutine after the replication flush and before `OnTickEnd`, forwarding the write result to the request's buffered channel; before `Run` it takes `preRunCaptureMu.RLock` while still holding `lifeMu`, releases `lifeMu` and captures with the ungated `saveSnapshot` on the caller (never under `lifeMu`, since `FullUpdate` is entity code; never through gated `SaveSnapshot`, whose second `RLock` could deadlock behind `Run`'s pending `Lock`), then releases the read lock; `SaveSnapshot` and `SaveContentSnapshot` take the same read lock through `enterPreRunCapture` when they observe `lifecycleCreated`, and `Run` takes and releases `preRunCaptureMu.Lock` after leaving `lifeMu` and before the listener and first tick, so a pre-Run capture never overlaps a tick; `drainSnapshotRequests` uses ungated `saveSnapshot`; once stopping/stopped it returns `ErrServerNotRunning` immediately, and `finalizeLifecycle` answers every still-pending request with `ErrServerNotRunning`; `SaveContentSnapshot` likewise captures spaces. `Server.LoadSnapshot(path, fingerprint string, restore func(snapshot.Record) (Entity, error)) error` calls `snapshot.Load`, iterates records, calls the provided restore function (pass the generated `synced.RestoreEntity`), calls `s.CreateEntityIn(rec.Space, ...)` for each (which binds `ServerBinder` entities before `OnSpawn` and registers schema colliders when helpers are installed), silently skips records whose type the restore function does not recognise, and advances `s.SetEntityIDCounter` to the highest restored entity ID. Runtime-only `Synced*.srv` is not part of snapshot bytes; `ApplyState` does not touch it.

**Generated Go server `Runtime` helpers** (`codegen/templates/go_server/shared.go.tmpl`): `Runtime.SaveSnapshot` / `SaveSnapshotOnTick` / `LoadSnapshot` wire `SchemaFingerprint` and `RestoreEntity`. `LoadSnapshotIfExists(path) (bool, error)` calls `LoadSnapshot` (single read/open via `snapshot.Load`/`os.ReadFile`; no separate `Stat` then open TOCTOU) and returns `false, nil` only when `errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist)`; other errors including `snapshot.ErrFingerprintMismatch` propagate. `RunSnapshotAutosave(ctx, path, interval)` is a blocking caller-owned loop: rejects nil `ctx` and non-positive `interval` before ticker/save setup, uses `time.Ticker`, checks `ctx.Err()` immediately before each save (so a buffered/ready tick does not start a save when already cancelled), waits for each `SaveSnapshotOnTick` result (tick-coherent; `golem.ErrServerNotRunning` once the server stops) before the next tick, returns save errors, and on cancel mid-save awaits that result then returns the save error if it failed otherwise `ctx.Err()`. A cancel racing after the pre-save check may still allow that already-starting save; in-flight saves are awaited. No hidden goroutine, logging, callbacks, or implicit final save. Unexported `startSnapshotAutosave` / `runSnapshotAutosave` accept injectable ticker/ticks/save for tests. Not emitted for non-Go-server clients.

**Boundaries:** `golem/snapshot` imports `golem/registry` (for `Entity`, `SpaceID`, `SpacedEntity`) and `golem/pb` (for encoding). It does **not** import `golem` (loop/API)—that import lives only in `golem/snapshot_api.go`. `golem/snapshot` does **not** import `codegen` or `schema`; fingerprint and `RestoreEntity` belong to generated code in the consumer package.
